The Mercedes-Benz GLA 200 CDI is primarily associated with the first generation (X156), produced from around 2013/2014 to 2017/2020 depending on the market. The "most recent version" applicable here refers to the latest iteration of the GLA 200 CDI within that generation. Specs and reviews largely point to the 2014-2017 model years.

Here's an evaluation based on the gathered data:

**Cost & Risk:**
*   **price_usd:** Around $47,900 AUD at launch in 2014. Used prices vary significantly. Compared to the current market average of $52,000, the original price was close to average, but as a used car now, it would be significantly cheaper. Scoring based on original price relative to 2014 market, adjusted slightly for its current used status. Let's aim for a slightly positive score reflecting its original positioning.
*   **total_cost_estimate_5yr_usd:** Mercedes-Benz maintenance can be higher than average. As a diesel and an older model, repair risk increases. Likely higher than the $40,000 average.
*   **insurance_usd_yr:** Insurance group 19E is cited. This is around the middle of the UK scale (1-50), suggesting average to slightly above average insurance costs.
*   **maint_usd_yr:** Mercedes maintenance is generally higher than average.
*   **repair_risk_score:** Mercedes-Benz has an average to slightly below average reliability reputation compared to top Japanese brands.

**Power & Performance:**
*   **hp:** 100 kW (134-136 hp). This is below the 200 hp market average.
*   **torque:** 300 Nm (221 lb-ft). This is around or slightly above the 190 lb-ft average.
*   **cylinder_count:** 4 cylinders. Average.
*   **engine_displacement:** 2.1 - 2.2 litres (2143 cc). Around the 3.0L average, but on the smaller side for a diesel.
*   **accel_0_60_sec:** 9.7 - 10 seconds. This is slower than the 7.5 sec average.
*   **top_speed:** 124 - 127 mph (200-205 km/h). Around the 150 mph average.
*   **drive_type:** Primarily Front-Wheel Drive (FWD), with optional 4MATIC AWD. FWD is -1.00, AWD is 0.00. Score based on the FWD variant.
*   **transmission_type:** 7-speed dual-clutch automatic (7G-DCT). Some manual options existed. Dual-clutch is 0.00.
*   **driving_track_vs_gt:** Described as a comfortable open road cruiser, not a sports car or sports SUV. More GT/daily driver focused.
*   **offroad_ability:** Described as having enough ground clearance for dirt roads but not serious off-roading, especially the FWD version. Higher than a sports car, but not a dedicated off-roader.
*   **braking_dist_ft:** One review mentions braking performs "beyond the call of duty". No specific distance found. Default to 0.00, maybe slightly positive based on the review.

**Space & Practicality:**
*   **size:** Length around 4412-4417 mm. This is slightly smaller than the 4600mm average.
*   **wheelbase:** Around 2699-2729 mm. Close to the 2700mm average.
*   **body_style:** 5-door SUV/Crossover/Wagon. Falls between sedan (0.00) and van (+1.00).
*   **seats:** 5 seats. Average.
*   **cargo_space:** 421-425 litres (14.87 cu ft). This is around the 15 cu ft average. Some sources cite 481 litres. Let's use the higher figure as it's also cited. 481 litres is about 17 cu ft. Slightly above average.
*   **rear_space:** Rear seat space is adequate, not ideal for tall passengers. Rear legroom claimed to be more generous in the 2nd gen, but this is the 1st gen. One source mentions 812mm (31.97 inches) rear legroom. This is below the 35" average.
*   **ground_clearance_in:** 183 mm (7.2 inches) with the bad road package. This is slightly above the 6.5" average.
*   **turn_radius_ft:** 11m (36.09 ft) or 11.84m (38.85 ft). Around the 36 ft average.

**Efficiency & Electrification:**
*   **mpg:** Claimed 4.6 L/100km (61.4 mpg US) or 62.8 mpg UK. Real-world around 6-10 L/100km (23.5-39.2 mpg US) or 17.5 kpl (41.2 mpg US). 17.9 kmpl (42.1 mpg US) also cited. Diesel engines are generally more efficient. This is above the 30 mpg average.
*   **co2_g_km:** 119-122 g/km. This is below the 175 g/km average.
*   **electrification:** Diesel engine. -1.00.
*   **electric_range_mi:** -1.00 (Not electric).
*   **charge_speed_kw:** -1.00 (Not electric).

**Ride & Dynamics:**
*   **handling_rating:** Handles pretty well, sure-footed, steering gives good feedback, but not sporty or fun as a Golf or Evoque. Steering lacks definition at straight-ahead but is well-weighted and accurate. Steering is sharp and responsive but lacks feel. Generally positive but not class-leading sporty.
*   **ride_comfort_rating:** Suspension is well-balanced, comfort is good, possibly better than A-Class. Ride is firm but not unbearable, glides beautifully on smooth roads. Ride is very good, far better than A-Class, manages to remain civil even on sports suspension. Generally positive.
*   **cabin_noise_rating:** Diesel clatter is audible on start-up, but settles down when cruising. Engine noise is shielded well, but coarser nature comes out when revved hard. Tyre noise is a downer on coarse surfaces. Diesel engine noise is quite audible in the cabin when revved. Mixed reviews, leaning towards noticeable noise under certain conditions.
*   **shift_smooth_rating:** 7-speed auto is good. Gearbox is fuel consumption-orientated, can lurch when needing to downshift quickly. Dual-clutch gearbox is prompt shifting up, but sometimes does its own thing shifting down, especially with the low red line. Mixed reviews, some complaints about low-speed or sudden downshifts.
*   **steering_feel_rating:** Steering gives good feedback. Steering could use more feel but weights up well. Steering lacks definition at straight-ahead but is well-weighted and accurate. Steering is sharp and responsive but lacks feel. Steering is a bit light, could use more feedback. Mixed reviews, generally accurate but lacking feel.

**Safety & Visibility:**
*   **adas_score:** Standard features include Attention Assist, Collision Prevention Assist, Active Brake Assist (includes forward collision warning and autonomous braking), Blind Spot Detection, Active Park Assist, Reversing Camera, Tyre Pressure Monitoring System. Optional features like Adaptive High-beam Assist Plus and Multibeam LED headlamps available. Good suite for its time.
*   **visibility_rating:** Rearward visibility is compromised by swoopy roof and angled tailgate. Elevated seating provides a good view. View of the road ahead is better than A/B-Class. Mixed, but rear visibility is a noted drawback.
*   **headlight_rating:** Automatic bi-xenon headlights with LED daytime running lights standard. LED headlights available. Bi-xenon or LED available, better than basic halogens.
*   **security_rating:** Engine immobiliser standard. Standard Mercedes security features.

**Infotainment & Connectivity:**
*   **screen_in:** 5.8-inch or 7-inch freestanding display. Optional 8-inch or 10.25-inch available. Aftermarket 10.25" and 12.3" screens exist. Standard screen size is small by today's standards.
*   **infotainment_ux_rating:** Centre screen looks like an afterthought but is easy to see and use. Operated via controller on centre console. MBUX system available on later models/facelift. Early system is functional but not cutting edge.
*   **smartphone_connect_rating:** Bluetooth connectivity with audio streaming standard. Apple CarPlay and Android Auto capability standard on later models/facelift. Early models had USB port. Scoring based on the availability of smartphone integration, even if optional or on later versions.
*   **audio_rating:** Six-speaker audio system standard. Harman Kardon sound system optional. Standard system is average, optional is better.
*   **usb_ports:** USB port in the centre armrest. Likely 1 or 2 standard.

**Comfort & Convenience:**
*   **comfort_feature_score:** Seat comfort package standard. Dual-zone climate control standard on Sport trim. Power seats with memory settings available. Heated/ventilated seats optional. Good range of features available, especially on higher trims.
*   **convenience_feature_score:** Active park assist, reversing camera, front/rear parking sensors standard on some trims. Easy pack tailgate optional. Keyless-Go optional. Good convenience features available.

**Style, Brand & Interior Character:**
*   **style_sleek_vs_boxy:** Sits midway between SUV and wagon, bold frontal style, strong swage lines, sleek integration of roof rails. More sleek than boxy compared to traditional SUVs.
*   **style_aggression_rating:** Aggressively styled stance, prominent badge, raised bonnet ridges, deeply creased sheet metal. AMG Line adds more aggressive styling. Moderate to high aggression depending on trim.
*   **style_modernity_rating:** Modern interior design. Looks similar to A-Class but with added attitude. Modern for its time.
